{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was the grandson of Bertrand of Toulouse and Pons Count of Tripoli on his father's side and King Baldwin II of Jerusalem on his mother's side. His early life was marked by the regicide of his father Raymond II who was killed by the Assassins in 1152. After his father's death Raymond III's mother Hodierna returned to Jerusalem and left the regency of Tripoli in the hands of Amalric I of Jerusalem who was then Count of Jaffa.\n\n*Reign* Raymond III became Count of Tripoli at a young age and was initially under the regency of Amalric I. He was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade. During his captivity Amalric I acted as regent for Tripoli. Raymond III was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid. Upon his release he married Eschiva of Bures the widow of Walter of Saint Omer and Princess of Galilee. He then became involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the succession crisis that followed the death of King Amalric I.\n\n*Succession Crisis* Raymond III was a key player in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was a leader of the faction that opposed the rule of Guy of Lusignan and supported the claim of Raymond's cousin Raymond of Antioch and later Conrad of Montferrat. The succession crisis ultimately led to the division of the kingdom and the loss of Jerusalem to Saladin. Raymond III died in 1187 probably of pleurisy at the age of 47. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his di